[Unit]
Description=Sofia PT archive compaction (gzip closed day files)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# Every *.jsonl except today's, today by Sofia's calendar. The name test
# covers <day>.polls.jsonl too. Sofia's calendar because collect.py rotates
# at local midnight: when this runs (00:00 UTC, 02:00 or 03:00 in Sofia),
# yesterday's file has been closed for two or three hours, since
# sink.rotate_if_needed() drops the descriptor on the first poll after
# local midnight. A day file must be closed before anything touches it,
# because its manifest describes the uncompressed bytes.
#
# The test was -mtime +2 before trip updates came in. That kept three or
# four plain days on the box, ~0.9 GB once collect.py wrote five more
# fields. An age test of -mmin +60 would catch today's file too whenever
# the collector had been down for over an hour past local midnight. The
# restarted collector then opens a fresh plain file for the same day,
# drop_superseded_plain.sh on the laptop sees two diverging copies and
# keeps both, the readers prefer the plain one, and the first hours of the
# day drop out of processing. A name test does not depend on how long
# anything was down.
#
# On the laptop, yesterday's plain copy pulled before midnight is now short
# of the .gz every day; drop_superseded_plain.sh drops it once the .gz
# holds all of its bytes and more.
#
# The shell is there for the date, then exec hands over to find. The [ -e ]
# guard matters: without the zone file glibc takes UTC without a word. The
# timer is Persistent=true, so a catch-up run after a reboot between 21:00
# and 24:00 UTC would take the UTC date, a day behind Sofia's, and gzip
# today's open file. With the guard the unit fails, and that shows. $$ and
# %% are systemd's escapes for $ and %. Europe/Sofia is spelled out
# because these units are Sofia's own; there is no pt-compress@ template.
#
# gzip rewrites the file in place (drops the original, keeps the same
# basename plus .gz), so nothing downstream needs to know which form a
# given day is in, see config.resolve_day_file() on the fetch side.
#
# -6 is spelled out rather than relying on gzip's own default (also 6) so
# this unit still does the right thing if that default is ever overridden
# system-wide via the GZIP env var. The disk estimates (~102 MB/day ->
# ~12 MB/day) were measured at this ratio.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c '[ -e /usr/share/zoneinfo/Europe/Sofia ] && exec /usr/bin/find /opt/sofia-pt/data -type f -name "*.jsonl" ! -name "$$(TZ=Europe/Sofia date +%%F).*" -exec /usr/bin/gzip -6 {} +'
User=root
